# en-GB-oxendict: only the messages whose words differ from the house English (en-GB).
# Everything else falls back to English.

# Help topic "Welcome to Trindle": caption and spoken name of picture 2 (ring-first.png).
settings-help-topics-welcome-pic2 = The ring, with the time, and date in the middle

# Help topic "Opening the ring and finding apps": paragraph 4.
settings-help-topics-ring-p4 = Round the middle sit your favourite apps, with Settings, Apps, and Folders always among them, and four tool circles drawn like apps: Screenshot (the camera), Clipboard, Notifications and the Magnifier. Click an app to open it, or to bring its window to the front. Programs that are open show in the ring of open windows round the favourites, not among the favourites.

# Help topic "Opening the ring and finding apps": step 1 of the numbered steps at block 7.
settings-help-topics-ring-step7-1 = Click All apps in the middle. Every app that is not already a favourite appears round the outside, in pieces: Internet, Office, Graphics, Sound, and video, Games and so on. A kind with no apps left has no piece.

# Help topic "Keeping your favourite apps in the ring": paragraph 2.
settings-help-topics-favourites-p2 = You start with Settings, Apps, Folders, Drives, and Activity. Any of them can be taken off, and put back from All apps.

# Words people might search for the Help topic "Switching between windows" by, in lower case, separated by spaces.
settings-help-topics-switcher-keywords = windows switch switcher alt tab open ring desktop show minimize clipboard copy paste history keyboard on screen

# Help topic "Switching between windows": paragraph 1.
settings-help-topics-switcher-p1 = Open the ring and your open windows are in a ring round the favourites, one part for each window, with its program's icon and its name. Point at one and the middle shows a picture of that window; click it to bring that window to the front, or right-click it for minimize, maximize and close. Alt and Tab also brings each open window to the front in turn.

# Help topic "Getting new apps": step 1 of the numbered steps at block 4.
settings-help-topics-apps-step4-1 = To remove an app, find it in Apps, and press the bin twice: the first press fills it with the highlight colour and shows a tick, and the second removes it.

# Title of the Help topic "Programs from their makers: Claude, Tailscale and more".
settings-help-topics-sources-title = Programs from their makers: Claude, Tailscale, and more

# Help topic "Programs from their makers: Claude, Tailscale and more": tip 4, shown in a box of its own.
settings-help-topics-sources-tip4 = Add another source takes any maker's source from their install instructions: its address, suite, components, and signing key.

# Help topic "Moving to another computer": paragraph 1.
settings-help-topics-moving-p1 = In Apps, choose Move your apps. Switch on what to take: your apps, their own data, your AppImages, and Trindle's settings. Press the save button and one backup file is made in your home folder; copy it to a USB stick or cloud storage.

# Help topic "Moving to another computer": paragraph 2.
settings-help-topics-moving-p2 = On the other computer, open the same page, press the open button, and choose the file. The apps are installed fresh from Flathub, and their data and your settings are put back. Log out and back in to see every setting.

# Title of the Help topic "Sound, speakers and microphone".
settings-help-topics-sound-title = Sound, speakers, and microphone

# Title of the Help topic "Sounds for logging in, the charger and the battery".
settings-help-topics-sounds-title = Sounds for logging in, the charger, and the battery

# Title of the Help topic "Bluetooth headphones, mice and keyboards".
settings-help-topics-bluetooth-title = Bluetooth headphones, mice, and keyboards

# Help topic "Taking a picture of the screen": paragraph 3.
settings-help-topics-screenshots-p3 = The picture is saved in Pictures, in a folder called Screenshots, named by the date, and time. It is also copied, so you can paste it straight into an email or a document, and the word Done shows for a moment at the screen edge, where notifications appear.

# Help topic "Having the screen read aloud": paragraph 1.
settings-help-topics-screen-reader-p1 = No screen reader is installed, so the switch in Settings, Appearance, under Accessibility, is greyed, and says so.

# Title of the Help topic "Colours, icons and text".
settings-help-topics-look-title = Colours, icons, and text

# Help topic "Themes: a whole look from one file": paragraph 1.
settings-help-topics-theme-files-p1 = A theme is a whole look, colours, corners, sizes, and room, kept in one file. Settings, Appearance, Theme shows every theme as a small picture drawn from its own colours. Trindle's own look, a deep blue night, is always first in the list and can never be removed. Midnight, which comes with Trindle, is black and grey with no blue in it, as Trindle's own look used to be.

# Help topic "Changing the look with a style sheet": paragraph 3.
settings-help-topics-theming-p3 = Every name you can use is in Trindle's own sheet, /usr/share/trindle/style.css, with what it does, and its limits beside it, such as (6 to 18). Changes for the light look alone go in a @media (prefers-color-scheme: light) { "{" } :root { "{" } { "}" } { "}" } block, as in Trindle's sheet. Shadows are written as a web page writes them, such as 0 8px 20px 2px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.8). Cards cast a gentle shadow to begin with; --trindle-card-shadow: none; makes them flat, and --trindle-edge: transparent; takes their fine edge away.

# Title of the Help topic "Folders, USB sticks and phones".
settings-help-topics-files-title = Folders, USB sticks, and phones

# Help topic "Folders, USB sticks and phones": paragraph 9.
settings-help-topics-files-p9 = Drives, in All apps, and in Settings, About this computer, shows every drive and how much space is free. From there you can open a USB stick, eject it safely before pulling it out, or format it. The drive the system runs on is never offered for formatting.

# Title of the Help topic "Older programs: Steam, Wine games and Java apps".
settings-help-topics-older-programs-title = Older programs: Steam, Wine games, and Java apps

# Help topic "Older programs: Steam, Wine games and Java apps": paragraph 1.
settings-help-topics-older-programs-p1 = Most apps on Trindle draw their windows the newer way. Some older programs, such as Steam, and its games, Wine games and Java apps, still draw them the older way, through a helper called Xwayland. On other desktops every older program shares one helper, so any of them can watch the others' windows and what you type into them.

# Title of the Help topic "People, passwords and pictures".
settings-help-topics-users-title = People, passwords, and pictures

# Title of the Help topic "Battery, sleep and shutting down".
settings-help-topics-power-title = Battery, sleep, and shutting down

# Words people might search for the Help topic "Keyboard shortcuts" by, in lower case, separated by spaces.
settings-help-topics-keys-keywords = keys keyboard shortcuts super windows key alt tab screenshot print volume brightness lock terminal close maximize screen reader desktop show

# Help topic "Keyboard shortcuts": step 1 of the numbered steps at block 2.
settings-help-topics-keys-step2-1 = Super on its own, or Super, and Space: open or close the ring.

# Help topic "Keyboard shortcuts": step 5 of the numbered steps at block 2.
settings-help-topics-keys-step2-5 = Super, Alt, and S: turn reading the screen aloud on or off.

# Help topic "Keyboard shortcuts": step 12 of the numbered steps at block 2.
settings-help-topics-keys-step2-12 = Print, or Super, Shift, and S: a picture of the screen, or of a part of it; Shift and Print: the whole screen at once.

# Help topic "Drives and USB sticks": tip 6, shown in a box of its own.
settings-help-topics-disks-tip6 = Formatting, resizing, and deleting ask twice, naming the drive and its size, and ask for your password. The drive the computer runs from is never offered for any of them. Erasing cannot be undone: copy anything you want to keep first.

# Help topic "Which app opens what": paragraph 1.
settings-help-topics-defaults-p1 = Settings, Default apps chooses which app opens web links, emails, pictures, music, videos, PDF files, and folders. Only apps that are installed and can open that kind of thing are offered.

# Help topic "About Trindle": paragraph 2.
settings-help-topics-about-p2 = GTK programs draw their own title bars. For the rest, such as the terminal, Qt programs, and older X11 ones, the compositor draws the title bar itself, with round buttons at the right as GTK's own windows have: a line to put the window away, a square to fill the screen, and a cross to close it.

# Help topic "About Trindle": paragraph 3.
settings-help-topics-about-p3 = Trindle is a rolling release: there is no timetable of big versions twice a year. Improvements come as they are ready, each with its own number, which Settings, About this computer shows along with the computer's processor, memory, graphics, and the rest. That page is also where the computer's name is changed, and it keeps the last ten start-up times, from the power button to the login screen, with the whole list saved to Downloads at a click. What the computer is doing right now is in Activity, a window of its own.

# Help topic "About Trindle": paragraph 5.
settings-help-topics-about-p5 = It is free software under the GNU General Public License, version 3: you may use, change, and share it. It comes with no warranty of any kind and is used at your own risk; keep a backup of everything that matters.

# Help topic "What is running, and why it is busy": paragraph 4.
settings-help-topics-activity-p4 = Down the side, four rings show the memory, the processor, the disk, and the swap file; on a computer with no swap file the last one shows the processor's temperature instead. In the memory ring, Trindle's own share is in the highlight colour, and your programs and Linux are in blue.

# Help topic "If something goes wrong": paragraph 2.
settings-help-topics-trouble-p2 = If the login screen ever fails to appear, or logging in keeps returning you to it, press Ctrl, Alt, and F2 together. An ordinary text login appears, where you can log in and fix things; Ctrl, Alt and F7 returns to the graphical screen.

